writing.prompt:
You are Andrea Quiroz, president of the Engineering Student Society at the Universidad Autónoma del Altiplano. Yesterday you sent this message to your classmates' WhatsApp group:

> Banda, malas noticias: la doctora Salas siempre no puede venir a la conferencia del jueves 15, se le complicó todo con un viaje. Ya hablé con la Coordinación y la movimos al martes 20, a la misma hora (5 de la tarde), en el Aula Magna. Porfa chequen si pueden y avísenme luego luego para apartar lugares. ¡Gracias!

(*Banda* = guys, very informal Mexican; *siempre no* = not after all; *porfa* = please; *chequen* = check, from *checar*; *luego luego* = right away.)

Now write a **formal e-mail** (180–220 words, the length of the DELE C1's formal letter or e-mail) to the director of the faculty, Dr. Ernesto Villaseñor Garza, telling him about the change and asking him to confirm whether he will attend.

Your e-mail should:

- have a subject line (*Asunto:*);
- open with a greeting that uses his title and ends with a colon;
- state your purpose, for example with *Me dirijo a usted para…*;
- give every fact: the speaker, the old and new dates, the time, the place and the reason;
- make the request politely, with the conditional or *Le agradecería que* + imperfect subjunctive;
- close with a courtesy formula and *Atentamente*, then your name and position;
- keep *usted* (with *le* and *su*) throughout and contain no colloquial words.

Laura's e-mail and *oficio* in the lesson are your models. Don't copy the message's slang: find a neutral or formal equivalent for each idea.
